1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,709
The chart showed 100. You pressed Buy.

2
00:00:09,158 --> 00:00:12,947
But the trade opened at 102. Where did those 2 go?

3
00:00:13,397 --> 00:00:18,387
Then you set a stop loss at 95, and the trade closed at 94.

4
00:00:18,837 --> 00:00:22,703
This is not the broker cheating. It is how the market works.

5
00:00:23,153 --> 00:00:25,033
Today we learn those rules.

6
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:30,787
Hello, this is episode three of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

7
00:00:31,238 --> 00:00:38,395
Last time we learned: the market is an auction where buyers and sellers both bid.

8
00:00:38,845 --> 00:00:48,720
Three questions today. What are Bid and Ask? Why is there a spread? And what do market, limit and stop orders really do?

9
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:54,552
Ever seen a currency exchange counter at an airport? There is a board there.

10
00:00:55,002 --> 00:01:00,410
We buy: 83 rupees. We sell: 85 rupees.

11
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:04,726
If you want a dollar, the counter sells it to you at 85.

12
00:01:05,176 --> 00:01:09,303
If you want to sell a dollar, the counter buys it from you at 83.

13
00:01:09,753 --> 00:01:14,586
One dollar, two prices. The 2 rupees in between are the counter's income.

14
00:01:15,036 --> 00:01:18,744
The market also always has exactly two prices like this.

15
00:01:19,744 --> 00:01:26,431
The first price is the Bid: the highest price someone is ready to buy at right now.

16
00:01:26,881 --> 00:01:34,039
The second price is the Ask: the lowest price someone is ready to sell at right now.

17
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:37,416
The Ask is always above the Bid.

18
00:01:37,866 --> 00:01:43,978
Buy right now and you pay the Ask. Sell right now and you get the Bid.

19
00:01:44,428 --> 00:01:52,134
And most charts show the Bid. That is why the chart said 100 and the trade opened at 102.

20
00:01:53,134 --> 00:01:57,104
The gap between Bid and Ask is called the Spread.

21
00:01:57,554 --> 00:02:00,794
Every trade starts with the loss of this spread.

22
00:02:01,244 --> 00:02:08,637
You bought at 102; sell that same moment and you get 100. A loss of 2 from the start.

23
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:13,841
Price must first cross this spread before you are in profit.

24
00:02:14,291 --> 00:02:20,090
That is why many tiny trades get expensive. You pay the spread every time.

25
00:02:21,090 --> 00:02:24,016
The spread is not always the same.

26
00:02:24,466 --> 00:02:30,656
When the market is crowded, like London and New York hours, the spread is thin.

27
00:02:31,107 --> 00:02:36,906
When few people are around, like late night or holidays, the spread widens.

28
00:02:37,356 --> 00:02:43,781
And right before big news, everyone steps back, so the spread suddenly jumps.

29
00:02:44,232 --> 00:02:49,875
So the spread is a thermometer of the crowd. Thin spread, busy market.

30
00:02:50,875 --> 00:02:55,629
Now orders. An order is simply an instruction you give your broker.

31
00:02:56,079 --> 00:03:01,147
Every order answers one question: do you want speed, or price?

32
00:03:01,597 --> 00:03:08,676
You cannot have both. That is where the three order types come from: market, limit and stop.

33
00:03:09,676 --> 00:03:14,352
A market order says: I want it now, whatever the price.

34
00:03:14,802 --> 00:03:20,287
A buy market order fills straight at the Ask. A sell fills straight at the Bid.

35
00:03:20,738 --> 00:03:26,144
This order never waits. It deals instantly with whoever is sitting there.

36
00:03:26,595 --> 00:03:30,226
Speed is guaranteed. The price is not.

37
00:03:31,226 --> 00:03:35,510
A limit order says: at my price, or not at all.

38
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:41,524
A buy limit sits below the current price. A sell limit sits above.

39
00:03:41,974 --> 00:03:47,225
It stands in a queue and waits for price to come to it.

40
00:03:47,674 --> 00:03:54,989
The price is guaranteed. The fill is not. Price can touch it and turn, before your turn in the queue.

41
00:03:55,989 --> 00:04:01,317
A stop order says: when price reaches here, turn me into a market order.

42
00:04:01,768 --> 00:04:08,769
A buy stop goes above, a sell stop below. Your stop loss is really a stop order too.

43
00:04:09,219 --> 00:04:17,108
Until price arrives, it sleeps. The moment price comes, it wakes up and runs as a market order.

44
00:04:17,558 --> 00:04:24,872
And a market order has no fixed price. That is why the stop was at 95 and filled at 94.

45
00:04:25,322 --> 00:04:35,353
Remember: where many stops sit together, price often runs fast. The full story in episode 17.

46
00:04:36,353 --> 00:04:42,388
The gap between the price you asked for and the price you got is called slippage.

47
00:04:42,838 --> 00:04:47,357
Why does it happen? Because only a limited amount is waiting at each price.

48
00:04:47,807 --> 00:04:53,292
Say only 10 lots are for sale at 102, and you want 30.

49
00:04:53,742 --> 00:05:00,351
So you get 10 at 102, the next 10 at 103, and the rest at 104.

50
00:05:00,801 --> 00:05:05,556
During news, there is even less waiting, so slippage is bigger.

51
00:05:06,556 --> 00:05:13,792
Now one trade, three ways. Bid 100, Ask 102. You want to buy.

52
00:05:14,242 --> 00:05:19,492
Market order: in instantly at 102. The cost you paid: the spread.

53
00:05:19,942 --> 00:05:29,111
Limit order at 98: price came down to 99 and ran up. Never filled. The cost: a missed trade.

54
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:38,391
Stop order at 110: the breakout came, the order woke up, but filled at 112. The cost: slippage.

55
00:05:38,841 --> 00:05:44,797
All three have a cost. The only question is which cost you choose.

56
00:05:45,797 --> 00:05:52,797
Now the most important part. A limit order puts supply in the market. It is the one who waits.

57
00:05:53,247 --> 00:05:57,453
A market order takes that supply. It is the one in a hurry.

58
00:05:57,903 --> 00:06:02,501
Remember episode one? Price is moved by whoever is in a hurry.

59
00:06:02,951 --> 00:06:09,220
So market orders move price. And limit orders become walls in their way.

60
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:13,381
Order Flow is really about watching this fight.

61
00:06:13,831 --> 00:06:19,317
Who is in a hurry, buyers or sellers? How big are their market orders?

62
00:06:19,767 --> 00:06:22,614
And how many limit orders are standing to stop them?

63
00:06:23,064 --> 00:06:28,471
Every tool ahead, delta or footprint, shows exactly this count.

64
00:06:29,471 --> 00:06:31,039
So, today's summary.

65
00:06:31,489 --> 00:06:39,116
You sell at the Bid and buy at the Ask. The gap is the spread, every trade's first cost.

66
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:47,769
Market order: speed guaranteed. Limit order: price guaranteed. Stop order: a market order at a trigger.

67
00:06:48,219 --> 00:06:55,220
Market orders move price, limit orders stop them. Order Flow is this fight.

68
00:06:56,220 --> 00:07:04,814
Next episode: the Order Book and Liquidity. How much is waiting at each price, and how market orders eat it.

69
00:07:05,264 --> 00:07:10,097
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

70
00:07:10,547 --> 00:07:16,659
For trade setups, our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in the next episode.

